@* Section II — Ethics *@
<p>
    Ethics is the study of right and wrong — the moral principles that let professionals interact fairly,
    honestly, and with respect. It is the second-largest topic on the exam at roughly <strong>20%
    (17–21 questions)</strong>, so it rewards careful study.
</p>

<h2 id="role"><span class="rref">II.1</span> The Role of Ethics in Society; Cultures &amp; Customs</h2>
<p>
    Ethics shapes the rules and traditions of a community and helps us tell right from wrong. Cultural norms
    influence how we perceive what is moral — different cultures may hold different beliefs about right and
    wrong — so context matters when judging conduct.
</p>

<h2 id="theories"><span class="rref">II.2</span> Ethical Theories &amp; Principles</h2>
<p>
    Ethical theories offer different lenses for navigating hard problems. None is inherently better than the
    others; each represents a distinct perspective and can lead to a different outcome. You do not need to
    memorize them, but you should recognize what each one emphasizes.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Mill’s Utilitarianism</strong> — the best option benefits the greatest number of people. Conflicts arise when weighing and distributing those benefits.</li>
    <li><strong>Kant’s Formalism (Duty Ethics)</strong> — the right choice is the one everyone could act on. Focus on the process and good outcomes follow — but a good process never justifies causing harm, and “white lies” are prohibited.</li>
    <li><strong>Locke’s Rights Ethics</strong> — the right decision respects the fundamental rights of all individuals, balancing rights when they conflict.</li>
    <li><strong>Aristotle’s Virtue Ethics</strong> — the right action strikes the correct balance. Powerful in principle, but vague and hard to apply.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Exam tip</span>
    <p>
        Use these theories to analyze a problem <em>before</em> the exam — during the exam there is no time
        to experiment. A worked example with analysis and a recommended decision appears on page 220 of the
        4th edition of <em>Canadian Professional Engineering and Geoscience: Practice &amp; Ethics</em>.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="codes"><span class="rref">II.3</span> Codes of Ethics of Engineers &amp; Geoscientists</h2>
<p>
    The <strong>Code of Ethics (COE)</strong> is a concrete guideline that clears up the ambiguity of ethical
    theory. Your Association enforces it and can discipline those who violate it. Its central rule is always
    the same: <strong>responsibility to the public comes before all other responsibilities.</strong>
    Memorization isn’t necessary — each Association’s code varies slightly and the NPPE is used by many
    Associations — but you should know the shared obligations:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Ensuring the protection of the public</li>
    <li>Disclosing conflicts of interest to employers</li>
    <li>Maintaining confidentiality for clients</li>
    <li>Following the “Golden Rule” when interacting with colleagues</li>
    <li>Upholding integrity, honesty, fairness, and objectivity toward employees and subordinates</li>
    <li>Preserving the honour, dignity, and reputation of the profession</li>
    <li>Looking after your own well-being — fair pay and a good working environment</li>
</ul>
<h3>The standards of conduct, in short</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Public safety comes first</strong></li>
    <li>Stick to what you know</li>
    <li>Be honest, fair, and objective</li>
    <li>Follow laws and rules</li>
    <li>Maintain the profession’s reputation</li>
</ul>
<p>
    When duties and interests conflict and the potential for bias is high, bringing in an impartial third
    party raises the ethical standard and builds trust in the decision.
</p>

<h2 id="dilemmas"><span class="rref">II.4</span> Common Dilemmas &amp; Making Ethical Decisions</h2>
<p>
    Always prioritize ethical considerations. Present your own recommendation confidently and step back
    unless there is a clear sign of illegality or unethical behaviour. When faced with something illegal,
    refuse to take part and offer a legal, ethical alternative instead. Breaching the Code of Ethics is never
    acceptable — refuse, and propose an alternative that upholds the code. Conflicts of interest must be
    disclosed transparently; if the conflict is significant, step down to preserve integrity and fairness.
</p>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">The EGAD method</span>
    <p>The ethics text recommends a practical technique for tackling dilemmas:</p>
</div>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Ethical issue</strong> — identify the primary ethical problems in the situation.</li>
    <li><strong>Generate alternatives</strong> — find options better than the one presented; don’t assume there are only two.</li>
    <li><strong>Analysis</strong> — assess every option against fairness, ethical theory, and the Code of Ethics.</li>
    <li><strong>Decision</strong> — pick the choice with the lowest negative impact.</li>
</ol>
